h whole-of-market consideration noted. (6) Key Features document [P8] explains Aviva Platform consolidation benefits and Pension Portfolio as a SIPP. (7) Pension switch report [P10] shows charge comparisons (new Aviva 0.6% vs retained Aviva 0.59% vs Zurich 0.29%). (8) Report content [P1] discusses CIP approach (diversification, active oversight, cost efficiency) and explains why investment pathways are not appropriate, distinguishing CIP from passive multi-asset fund solutions.","discrepancies":["Report asserts provider choice justified by 'lower costs' compared to existing pension; evidence [P10] shows new Aviva solution at 0.6% vs retained Aviva at 0.59% and Zurich at 0.29%, meaning new solution is not lowest cost among options shown","Report presents seven generic provider justifications (financial strength, choice, expertise, value, convenience, governance, innovation) as top-level rationale; none of these are client-specific and none are corroborated by comparative research in evidence — they are marketing attributes stated without alternatives considered or rejected","Report claims CIP is recommended; evidence [P1] discusses CIP benefits but the actual recommended portfolio [P4, P9] consists of two named funds (CT Universal MAP 50%, HSBC Global Strategy 50%), not explicitly presented as a CIP construct in the research documents","Report states funds 'have performed well in comparison with their peers' [canonical]; evidence [P3, P5, P11] provides raw performance data (CT 11.17% 1YR, HSBC 9.92% 1YR; CT 44.73% 5YR, HSBC 40.06% 5YR) but contains no peer comparison table or statement that these figures exceed peer benchmarks","Report describes investment choice rationale as linked to client wanting 'fairly simple' strategy and CIP providing 'enhanced oversight'; evidence [P1] contrasts CIP (active oversight, layered approach) with single multi-asset fund (simplicity) but does not establish which aligns better with the stated client objective, nor does evidence conf